{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nPydantic deliberately counts one Agent-visible model turn even when `FallbackModel` tries multiple candidates inside it. The approved RP-01 decision follows maintained-project practice: keep that request budget authoritative and make candidate activity operationally visible as tracing, not as `candidate_requests` in usage. Two distinct multi-attempt mechanisms exist and must not be conflated. MECHANISM A — `FallbackModel` candidate iteration inside ONE Agent run (built in `model_classes.build_fallback_model`, `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py:414+`; ordered provenance already recorded in `runner.PydanticAIRunner`, `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:372-465`). MECHANISM B — the native-to-prompted downgrade in `structured_run._pai_structured` (`src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:118-145`), which performs TWO separate `agent.run_sync` calls (native attempt, then on a schema-complexity rejection a prompted attempt) and therefore is two distinct Agent-request turns by design, not a FallbackModel candidate.\n\n## Approach\nInstrument Mechanism A: wrap each already-built concrete candidate model at the public Pydantic model boundary so candidate child spans nest beneath the existing Agent model-request span (the logical-turn parent, `pydantic_ai._instrumentation.open_model_request_span`, active when `setup_tracing`/`Agent.instrument_all` is on) via the ambient OpenTelemetry span context — pydantic-ai 1.107.2 exposes no public `InstrumentedModel`, so we reuse the same OTel tracer rather than a parallel system. Each candidate span records: stable zero-based `order`, provider-qualified model identity, an `outcome` drawn from a closed vocabulary {`returned`,`raised`}, and provider-reported usage only when the SDK response exposes it (else omitted, never inferred). Instrument Mechanism B at the `_pai_structured` orchestration boundary: mark the native attempt turn `raised` (with sanitized rejection reason) and the prompted attempt turn `returned`, honestly recording that the downgrade adds a second Agent run. Use the existing optional tracing setup so the base install stays import-clean and a missing exporter is a no-op. Do not alter `FallbackModel` selection, `ProviderSession` lifecycle, `RunUsage`, `UsageLimits`, pricing, or JSONL. Provider SDK/HTTP retries stay outside this candidate layer and are never inferred.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py` — wrap concrete candidate models at construction without changing order or fallback predicates (Mechanism A).\n- `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py` — mark the native-raised and prompted-returned downgrade turns at the `_pai_structured` boundary (Mechanism B); no change to downgrade eligibility or the two-run control flow.\n- `src/rebar/llm/tracing.py` — define the bounded candidate/turn span attributes (order, model identity, `outcome` vocabulary, optional usage) through the existing optional tracing seam.\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — pass candidate order/provenance into instrumentation without adding retry policy.\n- `tests/unit/test_llm_fallback_chain.py`, `tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py` — candidate order, outcome, provenance, and usage non-interference.\n- `tests/unit/test_candidate_tracing.py` — new focused module using an in-memory OTel span exporter.\n\n## Testing\n- Primary 503 then fallback success: one Agent request, two ordered candidate spans (`raised` then `returned`) beneath one model-request span.\n- All candidates fail: one `raised` span per attempted candidate and the existing ordered `FallbackExceptionGroup`.\n- Native-schema rejection then prompted success: TWO distinct Agent-request turns — native turn `raised`, prompted turn `returned` — confirming the downgrade adds a second run (not one turn with two candidates).\n- Tracing disabled: identical result, usage, imports, and exception behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Primary-failure/fallback-success emits two ordered candidate spans beneath one logical Agent model-request span, each carrying `order`, provider-qualified model identity, and an `outcome` in {`returned`,`raised`}, with the fallback model recorded as the answer; verified by an in-memory exporter test.\n- [ ] All-candidate failure emits one sanitized `raised` outcome per attempted candidate and preserves the existing ordered exception group and resolution classification; verified by fallback-chain tests.\n- [ ] Native-to-prompted downgrade emits two distinct Agent-request turns — native `raised` then prompted `returned` — at the `_pai_structured` boundary, adding the second Agent run by design while leaving fallback eligibility and usage unchanged; verified by a focused downgrade trace test asserting two model-request spans.\n- [ ] Candidate/turn spans contain no prompt, response, credential, endpoint secret, or unsanitized exception body when content tracing is disabled; verified by secret-canary assertions.\n- [ ] `_usage`, `UsageLimits`, pricing, and durable usage JSONL are identical with tracing on and off and contain no candidate counter; verified by paired-result assertions.\n- [ ] Importing and running the base installation without the tracing extra remains successful and creates no span dependency error; verified by the lean-import subprocess test."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786961954361015001,"uuid":"10b71291-f767-4cdd-9a2a-e921c1a8f153"}